                "text": "Let me understand the rule and the verifier.\n\nmostcolor = most common color. ofcolor gives indices of that color in the 3x3. multiply each index by the shape (3,3) to get top-left positions of 3x3 blocks. shift asobject(I) to those positions and paint onto 9x9 canvas.\n\nSo each cell (i,j) in input where color == mostcolor produces a copy of the whole 3x3 grid at block position (3i, 3j).\n\nWait, but positions are multiplied by shape = (3,3), so index (i,j) -> block starting at (3i,3j). So the block position corresponds directly.\n\nFor the example: input\n8 5 5\n6 6 6\n6 6 8\n\nmost color: 6 appears... 6 appears at (1,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,0),(2,1) = 5 times. 8 twice, 5 twice. Most = 6.\n\nSo blocks at positions where 6 is: (1,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,0),(2,1).\n\nBlock positions (multiplied by 3): (3,0),(3,3),(3,6),(6,0),(6,3).\n\nOutput shows copies at rows 3-5 cols 0-2, 3-5, 6-8; and rows 6-8 cols 0-2, 3-5. Yes matches.\n\nNow I need to create 3 demonstration pairs. Need inputs that verifier can evaluate. Must avoid ties in mostcolor? max(set(values), key=values.count) - tie breaking depends on set ordering, nondeterministic-ish but deterministic. Better to have clear unique most common color.\n\nBut also, must ensure the output keeps black holes at non-most-common locations. Let's design 3 distinct inputs.\n\nLet me create inputs with clear majority color.\n\nExample 1: all same color? Then output is 9 copies fully filled. That's valid and teaches something but maybe not holes. Better to have at least one distinct.\n\nLet's make varied ones.\n\nInput A:\n1 1 2\n1 2 2\n2 2 2\nMost color 2 (count: positions (0,2),(1,1),(1,2),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2) = 6). 1 count 3.\nBlocks at 2's positions: (0,2)->(0,6), (1,1)->(3,3), (1,2)->(3,6), (2,0)->(6,0), (2,1)->(6,3), (2,2)->(6,6).\n\nHmm but the copied grid includes color 1's and 2's. Fine.\n\nActually wait \u2014 the rule words say \"if the top, left box is blue then copy the entire 3x3 box into the top, left 3x3 area\". So block positions = input cell positions. Yes.\n\nLet me design three nice ones with distinct most colors.\n\nPair 1: mostly 4.\nInput:\n4 4 1\n4 1 4\n1 4 4\nCounts: 4 = 6, 1 = 3. Most 4.\nBlock positions for 4: (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2).\nGood spread.\n\nPair 2: mostly 7 with 2s.\nInput:\n2 7 7\n7 7 2\n7 7 7\nCount 7=7? positions (0,1),(0,2),(1,0),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2) = 7. 2 count 2.\nActually let me count: row0: 2,7,7 -> 7 twice. row1: 7,7,2 -> 7 twice. row2: 7,7,7 -> three. total 7. yes.\n2 count 2.\n\nPair 3: mostly 3.\nInput:\n3 9 3\n9 3 3\n3 3 3\nCount 3 = 7, 9 = 2.\n\nGood. Let's include variety: maybe one with all same color to show full? Not needed but could help. Actually distinct holes patterns is most informative. Let me do 3 with distinct hole layouts.\n\nActually the Student infers rule from 3 pairs. Good to cover: most color unique obviously. Let's submit these.\n\nLet me call show_transformed_input three times.",
